  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-222054",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =4{x}^{2}-40x+15 \\\\  & =4({x}^{2}-10x)+15 \\\\  & =4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2})+15 \\\\  & =4((x-5)^{2}-25)+15 \\\\  & =4(x-5)^{2}-100+15 \\\\  & =4(x-5)^{2}-85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=4(x-5)^{2}-85\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=4(y-5)^{2}-85\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4(y-5)^{2}-85 \\\\ x+85 & =4(y-5)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+85}{4} & =(y-5)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{x+85}{4}} & =y-5 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x+85}}{2}+5 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if $x\\ge 5$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\ge 5.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x+85}}{2}+5\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
